<section class="band" aria-labelledby="brief">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The brief</span>
      <h2 id="brief">Your ceiling is leaking. Where do you start?</h2>
      <p>A tenant arriving at a repairs page usually has a problem right now. They want to know if it's urgent, who to call and whether it's their job.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <div class="card card--soft">
        <p class="need-label">User need</p>
        <p class="need">“I want to quickly find out if this applies to me and what I need to do next.”</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card card--soft">
        <p class="need-label">Business need</p>
        <p class="need">Fewer calls asking how to report a repair or whose job it is, and fewer reports of tenants' own repairs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--surface" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">The original page</span>
      <h2 id="problem-title">Accurate and thorough, but organised around the organisation</h2>
      <p>I used the <a href="https://www.clydesdale-housing.org.uk/repairs/">repairs page from Clydesdale Housing Association</a> in South Lanarkshire. It's typical of small housing associations: useful information, added over the years, all on one long page. Here are its 7 sections, sized by word count.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <ol class="anatomy">
        {%- for s in anatomy %}
        <li>
          <span class="anatomy__label">{{ loop.index }}. {{ s.name }}<small>{{ s.need }}</small></span>
          <span class="anatomy__bar{% if not s.on %} is-off{% endif %}" style="--w: {{ (s.words / 880 * 100) | round(1) }}%"><span>{{ s.words }}</span></span>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ol>
      <ul class="key">
        <li><i aria-hidden="true"></i> Answers a tenant's question</li>
        <li><i class="is-off" aria-hidden="true"></i> About the organisation: 565 words, over a quarter of the page</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid--4" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">1</span><h3>The main action is buried</h3><p>How to report a repair comes second, after 185 words on repair categories. The out-of-hours number is inside a sentence.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">2</span><h3>“Click here”</h3><p>The online option is a link saying “Click here”, which means nothing out of context. It goes to a general forms page, not a repair form.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">3</span><h3>Labels, not tasks</h3><p>“Repairs Service” and “Service Level” name topics. They don't match the questions people arrive with, like “Is this an emergency?”.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">4</span><h3>About tenants, not to them</h3><p>“Tenant” appears 32 times and “you” only 7. “Tenants must report to us...” reads like a contract.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">5</span><h3>Long, passive sentences</h3><p>18 sentences run over 25 words, and around 30 use the passive voice, like “Repairs requests will be prioritised”.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">6</span><h3>Unexplained jargon</h3><p>“Responsive repairs”, “cyclical maintenance”, “fair wear and tear”, “SHQS” and “EESSH” appear without explanation.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">7</span><h3>An 880-word section</h3><p>Responsibilities covers whose repair it is, then 11 sub-sections from frozen pipes to Artex to gardens.</p></article>
      <article class="card"><span class="card__num">8</span><h3>Warnings in the middle</h3><p>That the association may force entry in an emergency, and won't put flooring back, are both buried inside long paragraphs.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="audit" aria-labelledby="audit-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Artefact 1 · Content audit</span>
      <h2 id="audit-title">23 blocks, 4 questions, one decision each</h2>
      <p>Tenants come to this page with 4 questions: is it an emergency, how do I report it, whose job is it, and how long will it take or will I pay? Content that answers one stays. Useful content that answers something else moves to its own page. The rest is cut from this page, but still linked.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="legend" aria-label="Key">
      <li><span class="tag tag--keep">Keep</span></li>
      <li><span class="tag tag--change">Rewrite</span></li>
      <li><span class="tag tag--move">Move or merge</span></li>
      <li><span class="tag tag--cut">Cut</span></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Content audit" tabindex="0">
      <table class="data-table">
        <caption>4 kept, 9 rewritten, 3 merged, 5 moved, 2 cut</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">#</th><th scope="col">Original block</th><th scope="col">What it says</th><th scope="col">User need</th><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">Where it went</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {%- for a in audit %}
          <tr><td>{{ loop.index }}</td><td>{{ a[0] }}</td><td>{{ a[1] }}</td><td>{{ a[2] }}</td><td><span class="tag tag--{{ a[3] }}">{{ a[4] }}</span></td><td>{{ a[5] }}</td></tr>
          {%- endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--soft" id="rewrite" aria-labelledby="rewrite-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Artefact 2 · The rewritten page</span>
      <h2 id="rewrite-title">Emergencies first, then how to report, then everything else</h2>
      <p>A concept rewrite, not the association's live page. Every fact comes from the original. Content that wasn't about getting a repair has its own page, linked at the bottom.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="browser">
      <div class="browser__bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span class="browser__url">clydesdale-housing.org.uk/repairs</span></div>
      <div class="browser__body">
        <article class="rewrite" aria-label="Rewritten repairs page">
          <h3>Report a repair</h3>
          <p class="lede">Tell us about a repair as soon as you notice it. Most repairs to your home are our job, and they're free.</p>

          <div class="emergency">
            <h4>If it's an emergency</h4>
            <p>Call us straight away on <strong>0808 175 6288</strong> (free). Outside office hours, call <strong>01555 435944</strong>.</p>
            <p>An emergency is something that could hurt someone or quickly cause serious damage. For example, a major leak, no electricity or an outside door you cannot lock.</p>
            <p>If you smell gas, call the National Gas Emergency Service on <strong>0800 111 999</strong>. If there's a fire or someone is in danger, call <strong>999</strong>.</p>
          </div>

          <h4>How to report a repair</h4>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="#rewrite">Report a repair online</a> (any time)</li>
            <li>Phone <strong>0808 175 6288</strong> (free), Monday to Friday, during office hours</li>
            <li>Email mail@clydesdale-housing.org.uk</li>
            <li>Visit our office, Monday to Friday, during office hours</li>
          </ul>
          <p>We'll ask what's wrong and where, when it started, when you're usually in, and a phone number our contractor can call. This helps us send the right person with the right parts, so we can fix it first time.</p>

          <h4>How quickly we'll fix it</h4>
          <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Repair timescales" tabindex="0">
            <table class="data-table">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Type of repair</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">When we'll come</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Emergency</strong></td><td>Could hurt someone or quickly cause serious damage</td><td>Within 5 hours, to make it safe</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Urgent</strong></td><td>Makes it hard to live in your home, and will get worse if we wait</td><td>Fixed within 2 working days</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Routine</strong></td><td>Everything else</td><td>Fixed within 7 working days</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h5>Your Right to Repair</h5>
          <p>By law, some small, urgent repairs must be fixed within a set time. This is called the Right to Repair. It covers things like a blocked toilet if it's your only one, no heating if you have no other way to heat your home, or an outside door that won't lock. If we're late, you can ask for a different contractor, and you might get compensation. <a href="#rewrite">Check which repairs are covered</a>.</p>

          <h4>Check if a repair is ours or yours</h4>
          <div class="grid grid--2">
            <div>
              <h5>Repairs we do</h5>
              <ul>
                <li>roof, outside walls, chimneys and gutters</li>
                <li>floors, inside walls, ceilings and stairs</li>
                <li>doors, window frames and catches</li>
                <li>boilers, radiators, pipes and water tanks</li>
                <li>sinks, baths, showers, toilets and taps</li>
                <li>wiring, extractor fans and door entry</li>
                <li>smoke and carbon monoxide alarms</li>
                <li>kitchen units, paths, fences and drains</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h5>Repairs you do</h5>
              <ul>
                <li>decorating inside your home</li>
                <li>small repairs, like sink plugs and door handles</li>
                <li>broken glass</li>
                <li>pest control</li>
                <li>lost or broken keys</li>
                <li>sweeping your chimney</li>
                <li>testing your alarms and changing batteries</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>We also repair damage from vandalism or a break-in, if you report it to the police within 24 hours. Before you do any DIY that could affect safety or the structure of your home, <a href="#rewrite">ask us first</a>.</p>

          <h4>When you'll have to pay for a repair</h4>
          <p>We'll charge you if you, someone you live with or a visitor caused the damage, if it came from DIY we didn't agree to, if you missed an appointment you'd agreed, or if we find damage when you move out. We call these rechargeable repairs. We'll tell you what the repair was and how much it cost, and you can ask to pay in instalments.</p>

          <h4>After you report a repair</h4>
          <p>Our contractor will phone you to arrange a time. If we need to lift your carpet or flooring, we'll tell you first. We can't put flooring back afterwards.</p>
          <p>In an emergency, if we can't reach you, we may have to force entry to make your home safe.</p>

          <div class="related">
            <h5>Related</h5>
            <ul>
              <li><a href="#rewrite">Looking after your home: frozen pipes, DIY, flooring and gardens</a></li>
              <li><a href="#rewrite">Planned work on your home</a></li>
              <li><a href="#rewrite">How we're performing</a></li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Content decisions</span>
      <h2 id="decisions-title">Why the page looks like this</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="grid grid--2">
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">1</span>
        <h3>Lead with the most urgent question</h3>
        <p>“Is this an emergency?” comes first, in a box that stands out. It has the association's emergency numbers, plus the gas emergency and 999 numbers, which the original didn't include. How to report comes next. Everything after that is reference.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">2</span>
        <h3>Cut 565 words, lose nothing</h3>
        <p>Satisfaction results and the planned maintenance section don't help anyone report a repair, so they left this page. Rules on frozen pipes, Artex and gardens moved to “Looking after your home”. Each one is still a link away.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">3</span>
        <h3>Link text that says where it goes</h3>
        <p>Every link makes sense read on its own, for example in a screen reader's list of links.</p>
        <div class="ba"><p class="ba__side ba__before"><span>Before</span>ONLINE: Click here</p><span class="ba__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><p class="ba__side ba__after"><span>After</span>Report a repair online</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">4</span>
        <h3>Talk to tenants, not about them</h3>
        <p>Writing to “you” in the active voice took the average sentence from 17.5 words to 11, and made the page sound like help rather than a contract.</p>
        <div class="ba"><p class="ba__side ba__before"><span>Before</span>Tenants must report to us, as soon as possible, any damage</p><span class="ba__arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span><p class="ba__side ba__after"><span>After</span>Tell us about a repair as soon as you notice it</p></div>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">5</span>
        <h3>A table, lists and no steps, each for a reason</h3>
        <p>Timescales are a table: 3 types, each with the same 2 facts. Whose repair it is are lists, grouped by part of the home, because people think “the boiler”, not “an internal repair”. Reporting is one action with 4 ways to do it, so it's a list, not numbered steps.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <span class="card__num">6</span>
        <h3>Terms I couldn't remove</h3>
        <p>Tenants will see “rechargeable repair” on letters, so I kept it but put the plain version first: the heading is “When you'll have to pay for a repair”, and the term comes at the end. “Right to Repair” is a legal scheme, so I explain it the first time it's used.</p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h3 style="margin-top:3rem">Headings people would actually look for</h3>
    <ul class="swaps">
      <li><del>Repairs Service</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>How quickly we'll fix it</ins></li>
      <li><del>Request a Repair</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>How to report a repair</ins></li>
      <li><del>Landlord &amp; Tenant Responsibilities</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>Check if a repair is ours or yours</ins></li>
      <li><del>Chargeable Repairs</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>When you'll have to pay for a repair</ins></li>
      <li><del>Access</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>After you report a repair</ins></li>
      <li><del>No heading at all</del><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><ins>If it's an emergency</ins></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--surface" id="steps" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">Artefact 3 · Alternative structure</span>
      <h2 id="steps-title">I also tried it as a step-by-step</h2>
      <p>Getting a repair done has a clear order, so I tested the content in the GOV.UK step-by-step navigation pattern.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.5rem">Get a repair done in your home: step by step</h3>
        <ol class="steps" role="list">
          <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">1</span><h4><span class="visually-hidden">Step 1: </span>Check if it's an emergency</h4><p>Something that could hurt someone or quickly cause serious damage. Call 0808 175 6288, or 01555 435944 out of hours.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">2</span><h4><span class="visually-hidden">Step 2: </span>Check if it's our repair or yours</h4><p>See which repairs we do, and when you'll have to pay.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">3</span><h4><span class="visually-hidden">Step 3: </span>Report the repair</h4><p>Online, by phone, by email or at our office. Say what's wrong and when you're in.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">4</span><h4><span class="visually-hidden">Step 4: </span>Let our contractor in</h4><p>They'll phone to arrange a time.</p></li>
          <li><span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">or</span><h4><span class="visually-hidden">Or: </span>If it's not fixed in time</h4><p>Use your Right to Repair to get a different contractor.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Why I recommend the single page</h3>
        <p>A step-by-step works best when a task spans several pages or services, like learning to drive, where each step is something to do somewhere else.</p>
        <p>Reporting a repair is mostly one action. Steps 1, 2 and 4 are a few sentences each, not separate tasks. As a step-by-step, people would click away to read 2 sentences and come back. On a single page, they scan the headings and read only what applies to them.</p>
        <p>The step-by-step does make the order obvious, especially checking whose repair it is before reporting it. So I kept that order in the single page's headings.</p>
        <p>I'd switch to the step-by-step if the association split its repairs content into separate pages, such as a repair form, a Right to Repair page and a responsibilities page. It would then tie them together.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="eyebrow">How I checked it</span>
      <h2 id="results-title">Measured, not guessed</h2>
    </div>
    <ul class="checks">
      <li><div><strong>Readability</strong><span>Both versions measured with the same script, using the Flesch reading ease and grade formulas on body text only.</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Heading outline</strong><span>The rewrite makes sense from its headings alone, with no skipped levels.</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Link text</strong><span>Every link on the new page makes sense out of context.</span></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Accessibility</strong><span>The page passes axe with no WCAG 2.2 AA issues, and works at phone width.</span></div></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-top:3rem">
